    cml:confidence "1.0000"^^xsd:decimal ;
    cml:curationStatus cml:Proposed .
doc:sec-135v a cof:Section ;
    cof:title "135V Power of inspectors to secure consumer goods" ;
    values:kind "section" ;
    values:originalText "If:  (a) an embargo notice relates to consumer goods; and  (b) the inspector who gives the notice believes on reasonable grounds that it is necessary to secure the consumer goods in order to ensure that the notice is complied with; the inspector may, during the embargo period for the embargo notice, do anything that the inspector thinks is necessary to secure those consumer goods (whether by locking them up, placing a guard or otherwise)." ;
    cof:pageNumber "168"^^xsd:integer .

concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-135w a cml:Concept ;
    skos:prefLabel "135W Power of inspectors to secure equipment used to supply"@en ;
    cml:realizedBy doc:sec-135w ;
    values:originalText "product related services  If:  (a) an embargo notice relates to product related services; and  (b) the inspector who gives the notice believes on reasonable grounds that it is necessary to secure equipment used to supply the services in order to ensure that the notice is complied with; the inspector may, during the embargo period for the embargo notice, do anything that the inspector thinks is necessary to secure that equipment (whether by locking it up, placing a guard or otherwise).  Other provisions  Chapter 6 Application of the Australian Consumer Law as a law of the Commonwealth  Part XI Search, seizure and entry  Division 6  Section 135X  Competition and Consumer Act 2010  Compilation date: 27/05/2026  135X  Consent to supply etc. embargoed consumer goods etc.  (1) If an embargo notice relating to consumer goods has been given, the owner of the goods or another person who has an interest in the goods may, in writing, request consent to do any of the following:  (a) to supply the goods;  (b) to transfer, move, alter, destroy or otherwise interfere with the goods.  (2) If an embargo notice relating to product related services has been given, the following persons may, in writing, request consent to supply the services:  (a) the person who would, but for the embargo notice, supply the services;  (b) another person whose interests would be affected if the services were not supplied.  (3) If a request for consent is made under subsection (1) or (2), the requested consent may be given, in writing, by the Commonwealth Minister, the Chairperson or any inspector.  (4) A consent given under subsection (3) is not a legislative instrument." ;
    skos:definition "product related services  If:  (a) an embargo notice relates to product related services; and  (b) the inspector who gives the notice believes on reasonable grounds that it is necessary to secure equipment used to supply the services in order to ensure that the notice is complied with; the inspector may, during the embargo period for the embargo notice, do anything that the inspector thinks is necessary to secure that equipment (whether by locking it up, placing a guard or otherwise).  Other provisions  Chapter 6 Application of the Australian Consumer Law as a law of the Commonwealth  Part XI Search, seizure and entry  Division 6  Section 135X  Competition and Consumer Act 2010  Compilation date: 27/05/2026  135X  Consent to supply etc. embargoed consumer goods etc.  (1) If an embargo notice relating to consumer goods has been given, the owner of the goods or another person who has an interest in the goods may, in writing, request consent to do any of the following:  (a) to supply the goods;  (b) to transfer, move, alter, destroy or otherwise interfere with the goods.  (2) If an embargo notice relating to product related services has been given, the following persons may, in writing, request consent to supply the services:  (a) the person who would, but for the embargo notice, supply the services;  (b) another person whose interests would be affected if the services were not supplied.  (3) If a request for consent is made under subsection (1) or (2), the requested consent may be given, in writing, by the Commonwealth Minister, the Chairperson or any inspector.  (4) A consent given under subsection (3) is not a legislative instrument."@en ;
    cml:integrityHash "1cbcf89c836b8b7112ac097d47d31c56a14a5e31430e6ce3b82ca734aab035aa" ;
    cof:pageNumber "168"^^xsd:integer ;
    cml:hasPart concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-135w-ss-1 ;
    cml:hasPart concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-135w-ss-2 ;
    cml:hasPart concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-135w-ss-3 ;
    cml:hasPart concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-135w-ss-4 ;
