concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-138c a cml:Concept ;
    skos:prefLabel "138C Transfer of matters by the Federal Court"@en ;
    cml:realizedBy doc:sec-138c ;
    values:originalText "(1) Subject to subsection (2), if:  (a) a civil proceeding instituted by a person (other than the Commonwealth Minister or the Commission) is pending in the Federal Court; and  (b) a matter for determination in the proceeding arises under this Part or the Australian Consumer Law; the Federal Court may, on the application of a party to the proceeding or of its own motion, transfer the matter, and any other matter for determination in the proceeding, to a court of a State or a Territory.  Other provisions  Chapter 6 Application of the Australian Consumer Law as a law of the Commonwealth  Part XI Jurisdictional matters  Division 8  Section 138D  Competition and Consumer Act 2010  Compilation date: 27/05/2026  (2) The Federal Court must not transfer a matter to another court under subsection (1) unless:  (a) the other court has power to grant the remedies sought before the Federal Court in the matter; and  (b) it appears to the Federal Court that:  (i) the matter arises out of, or is related to, a proceeding that is pending in the other court; or  (ii) it is otherwise in the interests of justice that the matter be determined by the other court.  (4) If the Federal Court transfers a matter to another court under subsection (1):  (a) further proceedings in the matter must be as directed by the other court; and  (b) the judgment of the other court in the matter is enforceable throughout Australia and the external Territories as if it were a judgment of the Federal Court." ;
    skos:definition "(1) Subject to subsection (2), if:  (a) a civil proceeding instituted by a person (other than the Commonwealth Minister or the Commission) is pending in the Federal Court; and  (b) a matter for determination in the proceeding arises under this Part or the Australian Consumer Law; the Federal Court may, on the application of a party to the proceeding or of its own motion, transfer the matter, and any other matter for determination in the proceeding, to a court of a State or a Territory.  Other provisions  Chapter 6 Application of the Australian Consumer Law as a law of the Commonwealth  Part XI Jurisdictional matters  Division 8  Section 138D  Competition and Consumer Act 2010  Compilation date: 27/05/2026  (2) The Federal Court must not transfer a matter to another court under subsection (1) unless:  (a) the other court has power to grant the remedies sought before the Federal Court in the matter; and  (b) it appears to the Federal Court that:  (i) the matter arises out of, or is related to, a proceeding that is pending in the other court; or  (ii) it is otherwise in the interests of justice that the matter be determined by the other court.  (4) If the Federal Court transfers a matter to another court under subsection (1):  (a) further proceedings in the matter must be as directed by the other court; and  (b) the judgment of the other court in the matter is enforceable throughout Australia and the external Territories as if it were a judgment of the Federal Court."@en ;
    cml:integrityHash "c07eaa4dbff31663a49dedcd4b18dafa8ca73e0426191058c0c5311c022d1481" ;
    cof:pageNumber "186"^^xsd:integer ;
    cml:hasPart concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-138c-ss-1 ;
    cml:hasPart concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-138c-ss-2 ;
    cml:hasPart concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-138c-ss-4 ;
