doc:sec-140e-ss-2 a cof:Section ;
    cof:title "140E(2) Commonwealth consent to conferral of functions etc. on" ;
    values:kind "subsection" ;
    values:originalText "(2) Subsection (1) does not authorise the conferral of a function or power, or the imposition of a duty, by an application law to the extent to which:  (a) the conferral or imposition, or the authorisation, would contravene any constitutional doctrines restricting the duties that may be imposed on the Commonwealth entity; or  (b) the authorisation would otherwise exceed the legislative power of the Commonwealth.  Other provisions  Chapter 6 Application of the Australian Consumer Law as a law of a State or Territory  Part XIAA  Section 140F  Competition and Consumer Act 2010  Compilation date: 27/05/2026" ;
    cof:pageNumber "202"^^xsd:integer .

concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-140e-ss-3 a cml:Concept ;
    skos:prefLabel "140E(3) Commonwealth consent to conferral of functions etc. on"@en ;
    cml:realizedBy doc:sec-140e-ss-3 ;
    values:originalText "(3) The Commonwealth entity cannot perform a duty or function, or exercise a power, under an application law unless the conferral of the function or power, or the imposition of the duty, is in accordance with an agreement between the Commonwealth and the State or Territory concerned." ;
    skos:definition "(3) The Commonwealth entity cannot perform a duty or function, or exercise a power, under an application law unless the conferral of the function or power, or the imposition of the duty, is in accordance with an agreement between the Commonwealth and the State or Territory concerned."@en ;
